  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 331 AM EDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nEarly morning GOES WV imagery and RAP analysis show zonal flow aloft \nwith a trough upstream over Manitoba and a closed low over Pacific \noff the coast of CA. High pressure ridging extends overhead at the \nsurface, keeping skies mostly clear and winds calm. Increasing cloud \ncover over the west will limit further cooling this morning, but the \ninterior central should settle a few degrees yet. Cool and calm \nconditions alongside weak southerly flow off Lake MI and ample low \nlevel moisture has resulted in patchy fog in the central UP with \nmore widespread fog coverage in Delta, Alger, and Schoolcraft \nCounties. Opted to hoist a SPS for areas of low visibility down to \n1/4 mile at times, holding off on any Dense Fog Advisory due to the \nsomewhat transient nature of the eastern fog noted on nighttime \nmicrophysics. Lows ahead of sunrise are expected in the mid 30s to \nupper 40s, coldest interior central. \n\nThe trough over Manitoba tracks east over northern Ontario and the \nUP today, sending a weak cold front across the area. Better forcing \nremains north of the CWA, but a low (15-25%) chance of showers is \nbriefly present, passing from west to east. Measurable precip, if \nany will likely be sparse and limited to a few hundreths at best. \nThe more likely scenario is a brief period of sprinkles under cloudy \nskies as winds shift northerly. Otherwise temps warm 10-20F above \nnormal into the 60s, save for upper 50s in the Keweenaw.\n\nThrough the remainder of the week, the deep trough off the coast of \nCA lifts northeast through the intermountain west to Saskatchewan, \nphasing with the upper level jet. This amplifies downstream ridging \nbuilding into the Great Lakes. As a result, the well above normal \n(+10-20F) temps continue into the weekend with widespread 60s to \neven low 70s in the west! With the drier airmass in place, tonight \nand Thursday night will only be slightly above normal in the 30s to \nlow 40s. Increasing low level southerly flow into the weekend keeps \nlows in the 40s to low 50s. Low level moisture and a strengthening \nsubsidence inversion could result in more widespread fog and low \nclouds developing tonight, lingering into much of the morning hours \nbefore lifting in the afternoon with diurnal heating and \nstrengthening southerly flow.\n\nAs a low pressure tracks north of the area this weekend, southerly \nflow will strengthen resulting in breezy conditions Saturday, \nfollowed light westerly winds behind the cold front Sunday. While \nthe air isn't much colder behind the initial front it will be \nnotably drier which could result in borderline elevated fire weather \nconditions on Sunday. While showers will accompany the front, they \nare expected to remain rather hit-and-miss and appreciable rainfall \nis not expected.\n\nEarly next week ensembles are trending towards a period of cooler \nweather that could lead to at least some lake effect precipitation \nand high temperatures closer to or below seasonal averages. The NBM \nhas a 40-75% chance for much of the UP to remain below 50F Monday \nand Tuesday, decreasing to 20-60% on Wednesday.",
